  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nRest of Today through Sunday...\n\nCurrently, temps rest in the upper 30s across Southern Indiana and \nCentral Kentucky. Patchy river valley fog is possible, and a pocket \nof freezing fog in a valley can't be ruled out due to below freezing \ntemps. A few upper-level clouds will likely linger through much of \nthe day. Expect highs today in the upper 60s and calm winds.\n\nSurface high pressure lingers in the Ohio Valley through Saturday \nmorning before entering a more zonal pattern aloft. Expect highs in \nthe upper 60s through Sunday and overnight lows in the upper 30s on \nThursday and Friday nights; a bit of a warm nudge on Saturday night \ninto the upper 40s ahead of the advancing front.\n\nSunday afternoon and evening, an upper-level shortwave trough pushes \nthrough. Scattered rain showers and a weak surface cold front will \naccompany it. No thunder is expected due to a lack of instability, \nand less than .25\" of total precip will occur within the showers. \nSouthern Indiana has a 40% chance of seeing more than .25\",  while \nthe I-65 corridor and east has less than a 25% chance of the same \namount. Sunday night's lows occur in a gradient from upper 30s in S \nCentral Indiana to mid 40s in the east-central KY and Lake \nCumberland regions.\n\nMonday through Thanksgiving...\n\nZonal flow aloft persists early next week, keeping us dry. Temps \nwill also stagnate, with highs on Monday in the mid-50s and in the \nmid-40s for the rest of the forecast period. Overnight lows rest in \nthe upper 20s and low 30s. \n\nOur next shot at moisture comes Wednesday into Thursday as an upper-\nlevel trough ejects toward Alabama and Mississippi. There is \ncurrenlty low confidence in the strength of this system and \nassociated precip, as model guidance diverges at the end of the runs \nand inconsistencies between runs are plentiful.",
